  "response": "Identification of the suspected agent is itself contested. Phytophthora cinnamomi is a soil-borne oomycete pathogen of woody plants. Phytophthora cinnamomi is a true fungus belonging to the phylum Ascomycota. Placement in the Ascomycota cannot be squared with membership of the oomycetes, since no organism can sit in both lineages, and the laboratory routines that follow from the two placements, selective baiting on the one hand and conventional mycological culture and keys on the other, are incompatible in the same way.\n\nInvestigators baited rhizosphere soil samples with leaf tissue in order to isolate Phytophthora species. Either Phytophthora cinnamomi was recovered from at least one soil sample taken in the stand, or no soil sample taken in the stand yielded Phytophthora cinnamomi; one of those two outcomes holds, and they cannot both hold. After the baiting had been carried out, qPCR assays detected Phytophthora DNA in soil collected beneath dying trees. That detection shows that the pathogen was present in the soil of the affected stand. At least one of two conditions is true of the site, and perhaps both of them: the pathogen was present in its soil, or the stand received below-average rainfall in the three years before the dieback. Tree-ring analysis quantified radial growth reductions in the years preceding the dieback. Annual growth rings in oak cannot be used to date past stress events. If oak rings carried no datable record of stress, measured reductions could never have been assigned to particular years, so the dated growth losses and the denial that rings can date stress cannot both be true. Whereas the rainfall shortfall of the three preceding years was regional in extent and fell on sound and declining trees alike, symptom mapping showed mortality clustered along a recently constructed access track, and that clustering indicates that the pathogen was present in the soil of the affected stand, the track having served as a conduit for infested earth and run-off.\n\nAttribution is completed by reading the sequence of injury in the trees themselves. Fine root loss was recorded on the root systems of symptomatic oaks, and the destruction of the absorbing roots led bark beetles and Armillaria to colonise trees already weakened in the stand. Such secondary colonisers are not in themselves diagnostic, since chronic water deficit and infection of the absorbing roots both leave an oak open to them; the discriminating evidence therefore lies in the condition of the fine roots and in where the dying trees stand relative to likely routes of inoculum movement.\n\nThe dieback under investigation struck a stand that consisted of mature Quercus robur trees, and the two explanations put forward were an introduced soil-borne pathogen and prolonged drought stress. Pathologists treat such a case as a problem of discrimination: each candidate predicts a different signature in the soil, in the root system, in the growth record and in the spatial distribution of mortality, and the work consists in finding observations that one account accommodates and the other does not.",
